This document briefly describe the purpose of each utility. More details can be found inside each script,
most of which also have a "--help" option that explains their functionalities

- create_vasp_folder.py: automatically create folders for vasp simulations starting from POSCAR files (they just need 
  to have the same extension and POTCAR files named POTCAR_<element_name>.  
  Typical usage: to create phase diagram with specific simulation settings (e.g. DFT functional) and/or combining structures from
  different databases

- filter_Eform.py: post-processing filter for the IPSE_DB module, filters out high-energy polymorphs. 
  Typical usage: to save computational resources hen wants to recalculate a phase diagram with their own computational settings
  and/or combining structures from different databases

- grep_energies.py: goes through all folders and subfolders, look for vasp files, and prints: 
  folder name, total formula, minimal Hill formula, total energy, total energy per atom.
  Typical usage 1: easily extract final energies from vasp simulatons
  Typical usage 2: create a file for IPSE_CH to generate a convex hull with the results of vasp simulations   

- check_duplicates.py: from a set of (possibly tightly-converged) vasp simulations, finds structures that are duplicate of 
  each other, groups them, and prints a script to copy unique structures in a separate folder 
  Typical usage: if a long list of compounds was retrieved from different databases, one performs low-accuracy 
  but tightly converged simulations and exclude duplicates before performing accurate, 'production' simulations 

- check_vasp.py: highly useful to handle many vasp simulations. It checks that every simulation finished correctly
  by inspecting the vasp files inside each simulation folder. It then prints a report. Not so fast for hundreds of 
  folders (like 10-20 min for 400-700 folders)
  Typical usage: inspect all simulations, restart those that didn't converge, manually change incar files in those crashed 
  
- custodian_HTvasp.py: a wrapper of cuctodian* to automatically handle a VASP simulation, including automatic error handling. 
  To use this for high-throughput, combine it with scripts for habdling array of jobs (e.g. in slurm)     
  *https://github.com/materialsproject/custodian

- vasp_mongo.py: independent transfer of VASP results between folders and MongoDB.
  Edit the settings at the top of the script, then run: python utilities/vasp_mongo.py
  action="import": scan root_dir down to max_depth; read the last F/TOTEN (or E0)
  from OUTCAR, falling back to OSZICAR; read CONTCAR, falling back to POSCAR.
  POSCAR fallback is reported because it may be the initial structure.
  Stores struct, formula, elements, E_total (eV/cell), E_per_atom (eV/atom),
  source files/folder, energy convention, and termination-marker status.
  # 2026-10-17: Folder-derived import IDs and explicit collision/update policy.
  Import IDs join the last id_folder_levels folders (default 2) with "__".
  Optional id_prefix adds a project namespace. Host and leading path do not affect IDs.
  Existing IDs are rejected unless update_existing=True; updates preserve created_at.
  This utility does not compute formation energies or convergence.
  action="export": write POSCAR_<label> files and energies.csv (label, chosen energy).
  # 2026-10-18: Include stored formulas in export labels, independently of import options.
  Labels are <formula>_<MongoDB-ID> when formula is a nonempty string, otherwise <MongoDB-ID>.
  POSCAR_<label> and energies.csv use the same label; the POSCAR comment also matches.
  IDs containing path separators or control characters are skipped with a message.
  Set elements=["Pb","S","Cl"] with element_selection="subset" for compounds made
  only of these elements (including lower binaries); "exact" requires all and only
  those species; "contains_all" permits additional species. Empty elements allows all.
  excluded_elements and mongo_filters provide additional export filtering.
  Change export_energy_field and mongo_structure_field for existing collections;
  nested field paths and structures stored as POSCAR text are also supported.
  Existing export files are protected unless overwrite=True. All options, including
  Mongo server/database/collection, are at the beginning; no input module is needed.
  Dependencies: pymatgen (including monty) and pymongo. No ML environment is required.
  Offline regression checks: python -m unittest utilities.test_vasp_mongo
  # 2026-10-17: Optional VASP file archival through collection-specific GridFS buckets.
  upload_incar=True stores INCAR unchanged; upload_oszicar=True stores OSZICAR.gz;
  upload_outcar=False can be enabled to store OUTCAR.gz. Missing files are skipped.
  GridFS uses <collection>_files.files and <collection>_files.chunks in the same DB.
  Records contain files references, original/stored sizes, compression and SHA256.
  Explicit updates reuse identical attachments and delete replaced blobs after linking.
  Disabled/missing files leave existing attachments intact. File errors print warnings
  while retaining the imported structure/energy; inspect these warnings after imports.
  export_original_files=True restores attachments to export_dir/<MongoDB-ID>/,
  preserving gzip; POSCAR_<label> and energies.csv remain in export_dir.
  Export verifies stored checksums and respects overwrite for attachment files too.
  # 2026-10-18: Optional exports from incomplete Mongo records (both default False).
  export_energy_without_structure=True permits CSV energy rows without a POSCAR.
  export_structure_without_energy=True permits POSCARs without an energy CSV row.
  Missing, null, or invalid counterparts produce PARTIAL messages; unavailable values
  are never fabricated. Records lacking both usable results are skipped.
  Element filtering of energy-only records requires valid elements metadata; records
  with unknown composition are skipped when elements/excluded_elements filters apply.
  Stored attachments are also retrieved for accepted partial records when enabled.
